{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The board should ask management to investigate:\n\n- **Valuations:** Is the ETF’s closing price a trade, quote midpoint or auction? Synchronize price/NAV timing, market hours, corporate actions and fair-value treatment; recalculate apparent gaps.\n- **Peers:** Compare deviations after accounting for benchmarks, spreads and valuation methods.\n- **Frictions:** Review constituent liquidity, halts, spreads, short availability, hedging costs, settlement constraints, basket composition and creation/redemption fees.\n- **Arbitrage:** Assess actual AP activity—not just signed agreements—creation/redemption flows, rejected orders, dealer capacity, market-maker quote quality and concentration. Ask participants whether apparent opportunities remain profitable after costs.\n\nDepending on findings, consider valuation/data corrections, basket/fee/process changes, additional APs or market makers, and exchange discussions about closing liquidity. Monitor persistence, executable deviations and investor costs.\n\n**Worth knowing:** A recurring closing gap warrants investigation; it neither proves arbitrage failure nor constitutes an automatic numerical trigger."}}
